          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.603,
            0.044,
            0.848,
            0.061
          ],
          "content": "Describing Motion Around Us"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 65"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.087,
            0.773,
            0.123
          ],
          "content": "Using these equations, it is possible to predict position or velocity of the object at a future time."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.128,
            0.214,
            0.144
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.147,
            0.744,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "These kinematic equations are valid only when the acceleration is constant. While using kinematic equations for motion in a straight line in one direction, remember that distance travelled and magnitude of displacement are equal, and speed and magnitude of velocity are also equal. In motion in a straight line in both directions, the sign of $u , v , a ,$ and s in these equations tells us about the direction of that particular quantity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.261,
            0.775,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4.8:** Suppose a car is moving on a highway and brakes are applied, which cause an acceleration of – 4 m $\\overline { { S } } ^ { - 2 }$ . How much will be the distance travelled by the car before coming to a stop, if the car was moving with a velocity of (i) 54 km $\\mathrm { h ^ { - 1 } }$ , and (ii) 108 km $\\mathrm { h } ^ { - 1 }$ when the brakes were applied?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.351,
            0.195,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": "**Answer:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.37,
            0.378,
            0.387
          ],
          "content": "Given: a = – 4 m ${ \\cal S } ^ { - 2 } ,$ v = 0 m $S ^ { - 1 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.39,
            0.662,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": "Suppose the initial velocity is u and the distance travelled is s."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.411,
            0.342,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "(i) u = 54 km h<sup>–1</sup> = 15 m s<sup>–1</sup>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.432,
            0.358,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $u = 108    km     h ^{-1} = 30    m     s ^{-1}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.453,
            0.252,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "Using Eq. (4.4c)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.473,
            0.259,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": "\\nu ^ { 2 } = u ^ { 2 } + 2 a s",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.494,
            0.428,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "( 0 \\; \\mathrm { m } \\; \\mathrm { s } ^ { - 1 } ) ^ { 2 } = u ^ { 2 } + 2 \\times ( - 4 \\; \\mathrm { m } \\; \\mathrm { s } ^ { - 2 } ) \\times s",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.514,
            0.421,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "0 = u ^ { 2 } - 8 \\times s \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad s = \\frac { u ^ { 2 } } { 8 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.551,
            0.682,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": "Substituting the value of $u _ { \\flat }$ we obtain (i) 28.1 m, and (ii) 112.5 m."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.569,
            0.175,
            0.616
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.583,
            0.484,
            0.603
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.615,
            0.774,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "When brakes are applied to a moving vehicle, it moves some distance before coming to a stop. The distance travelled depends upon the velocity of the vehicle when the brakes are applied, the road surface (wet/dry, etc.), the braking capacity of the vehicle (the negative acceleration caused by the brakes) as well as the driver’s reaction time. Can you now understand why it is important to maintain a safe distance from the vehicle moving ahead of your vehicle (Fig. 4.20) and how this distance needs to be adjusted given your initial velocity?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.731,
            0.298,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.591,
            0.732,
            0.77,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.266,
                0.782,
                0.642,
                0.798
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.20: Safe distance between two moving vehicles"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.801,
            0.774,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": "There is a vehicle-to-vehicle (V2V) communication technology, now being developed in many countries including India, which allows vehicles to exchange signals and warns drivers of possible collisions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.873,
            0.774,
            0.927
          ],
          "content": "Till now, in this chapter, we have been discussing about motion in a straight line which is also called motion in one dimension. Let us now explore motion in a plane."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
